# OWNER-LOGIN DEPLOYMENT — Compose INTERPOLATION values for docker-compose.image.yml and its overrides
# (docker compose --env-file <this file>). The ONLY secrets here are the two database values below (DB-SETUP, like
# Twenty: compose builds the bundled db and its admin URL from them; no container ever receives PG_DATABASE_PASSWORD, and
# only the one-shot db-setup service receives PG_DATABASE_URL, PR #125 option 2);
# otherwise only image references, host paths, the API bind address/port and resource limits. Copy to e.g.
# /etc/crewflow/compose.env (root:root 0600). Passing --env-file also stops Compose from reading a stray .env next to the
# compose files.
# Lint it: node packages/rab-docker/scripts/compose-owner-login.cjs --lint-env-file compose /etc/crewflow/compose.env
#
# DATABASE (DB-SETUP, owner decisions D1 a / D4 / D5; PR #125 option 2). REQUIRED: the bundled db's superuser password,
# 32+ URL-safe characters (generate it: openssl rand -hex 32). It is read only on the db volume's first start; changing it
# later needs ALTER ROLE postgres in the db container first. With it alone, PG_DATABASE_URL defaults to the bundled db's
# superuser and the one-shot db-setup service creates crewflow_owner (its password derived from APP_SECRET): no SQL step.
PG_DATABASE_PASSWORD=<openssl-rand-hex-32>
# PG_DATABASE_URL: the ONE database setting. Leave it unset for the bundled db. For your OWN database (the bundled db then
# runs idle), put ONE of these here, at the DIRECT host (never a transaction pooler: Neon -pooler, Supabase :6543,
# DigitalOcean :25061, RDS Proxy, PgBouncer :6432), sslmode=verify-full:
#   - its ADMIN URL: the login must be able to create roles (CREATEROLE or superuser) and to grant on the database;
#     db-setup creates crewflow_owner (password derived from APP_SECRET); or
#   - an existing crewflow_owner URL (made with postgres-init/owner-login/01-crewflow-owner.sql; DB-SETUP-PLAN §7.1):
#     db-setup checks it (never SUPERUSER / BYPASSRLS, PostgreSQL 16+) and, the first time, sets crewflow_owner's own
#     password to the one derived from APP_SECRET; the password in this URL is then no longer used.
# Only the one-shot db-setup service receives it; rab-api and rab-worker never do (they build the crewflow_owner URL from
# db-setup's credential-free target file and APP_SECRET). No psql, no manual step, no second variable.
# PG_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://<admin-login-or-crewflow_owner>:<password>@<db-direct-host>:5432/<db-name>?sslmode=verify-full
#
# Image: the approved release's multi-platform INDEX digest (amd64 + arm64). The variable is named like Twenty's TAG but
# accepts ONLY a digest (sha256:<64 hex>); a plain tag is refused. ONE image, ONE variable runs the API (which migrates on
# start, O3), the Worker and the owner CLI one-off commands run from rab-api (O1/O2, X2.2; published under the one tag
# <full commit>; take the digest from the release manifest). Deploy: `docker compose pull && docker compose up -d`.
# Rollback: put the previous digest here, same command.
# Refused here: CREWFLOW_IMAGE_DIGEST (this variable's name until 2026-11-11) and any per-role variable
# (CREWFLOW_API_IMAGE_* / CREWFLOW_WORKER_IMAGE_*): there is no API-only or Worker-only deploy.
CREWFLOW_IMAGE_TAG=sha256:<image-index-digest-64-hex>
# Only to override the release registry (default adhin007/adolphus):
# CREWFLOW_IMAGE_REPOSITORY=<registry/repository>
#
# Migration approval (BC-11, C4): unset = none, the API applies pending migrations on start without a token (like
# Twenty). token = opt-in safety net: an API start that would apply to a non-fresh database needs
# CREWFLOW_MIGRATION_APPROVED=<token> for exactly the pending set (passed to that one start only, never in a file). Set it
# HERE only: the lint refuses it in database.env / runtime.env, where rab-api's committed value would override it.
# CREWFLOW_MIGRATION_APPROVAL_MODE=token
# Redis 7.x, pinned by digest; record the tag it was resolved from.
CREWFLOW_REDIS_IMAGE=redis@sha256:<redis-7-alpine-index-digest-64-hex>
# Only to override the bundled db's committed image (postgres:16, Debian, by digest): another Debian postgres:16, :17 or :18 digest (never alpine: the volume layout is the Debian one).
# CREWFLOW_POSTGRES_IMAGE=postgres@sha256:<postgres-index-digest-64-hex>
#
# Host paths (absolute). The secret directory (root 0700) holds database.env, runtime.env and redis-users.acl.
CREWFLOW_SECRETS_DIR=/etc/crewflow/secrets
# EXTERNAL database only, when its CA is not in the system store: the provider's CA bundle (mounted at
# /run/crewflow/pg-ca.crt; set PG_SSL_CA_CERT_PATH to that path in database.env). The bundled db needs none.
# CREWFLOW_PG_CA_FILE=/etc/crewflow/pg-ca.crt
#
# The address and host port the edge proxy connects to. Never 0.0.0.0. Pick a free host port when another service already
# uses 3000 on this host (Oracle VM: OWNER-LOGIN-ORACLE-VM-PLAN.md).
CREWFLOW_API_BIND=127.0.0.1
CREWFLOW_API_HOST_PORT=3000
#
# Resource limits (cpus must not exceed the host's vCPU count; Docker refuses to start the container otherwise).
CREWFLOW_API_MEM_LIMIT=1024m
CREWFLOW_API_CPUS=2
CREWFLOW_WORKER_MEM_LIMIT=1536m
CREWFLOW_WORKER_CPUS=2
CREWFLOW_REDIS_MAXMEMORY=384mb
CREWFLOW_POSTGRES_MEM_LIMIT=1024m
CREWFLOW_POSTGRES_CPUS=2
#
# MUST NOT BE SET here: any other password, URL, token (CREWFLOW_MIGRATION_APPROVED included) or secret, and any
# application variable (NODE_ENV, other PG_*, REDIS_URL, APP_SECRET, CREWFLOW_DB_MODE, ...). COMPOSE_FILE / COMPOSE_PROFILES /
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME: name the files explicitly with -f instead.
